b['shopping-21']['mistake'].update(alternative=True,why='Ze betaalde means she paid and is grammatical for one woman. The intended subject here is they, requiring plural betaalden.')
b['leisure-13']['mistake'].update(alternative=True,why='Kijken naar is the neutral model taught here. Watching media or sport also allows direct-object kijken, such as een film kijken and voetbal kijken. Zij keken de wedstrijd can therefore describe watching the match, especially as a broadcast; it is not categorically ungrammatical.')
b['conversation-66']['mistake'].update(alternative=True,why='The model uses the ordinary infinitive clause (om) te helpen. Dutch also has evaluative bare-infinitive expressions, so do not treat every adjective followed by a bare infinitive as automatically impossible. Practise the model for this neutral advice.')
b['conversation-57']['mistake']['why']='The displayed phrase omits te and does not fit this request complement. Use om vroeg te komen or vroeg te komen; om can be left out, but te is retained.'
b['conversation-61']['mistake']['why']='The person receiving the call is an object, so use me or stressed mij. Ik is a subject form. Pronoun choice depends on its grammatical role, not simply being after any verb.'
b['conversation-65']['mistake']['why']='Use goed with singular indefinite neuter advies here. Definite het goede advies and plural goede adviezen do take -e; the rule depends on the whole noun phrase.'
b['school-18']['mistake']['why']='Keep the short object het and recipient mij before the participle in this neutral clause. Uitgelegd closes the verb bracket. This is not a ban on full prepositional phrases after participles in other sentences.'
b['weather-13']['mistake']['why']='For looking at the sky use naar de lucht. Kijken can have a direct object in certain media expressions such as een film kijken, but de lucht does not fit that pattern.'
b['conversation-70']['mistake'].update(alternative=True,why='Bang van is also accepted and is especially common in Belgium. Bang voor is the model here and more usual in the Netherlands. Do not treat the alternative preposition as a universal error.')

# Full-record inspection, grammar positions 260–319; 263 remains pending.
fixes.update({
 'work-26':{'forms':'Gaat is finite with singular ze; vinden remains an infinitive without te. Gaan + infinitive can predict an event as well as express an intention. The model predicts finding a job soon, not merely looking for one. Baan is a de-word, so use een nieuwe baan.','compare':'English is going to find corresponds to gaat ... vinden. Binnenkort means soon; neither tense form proves that the future event is guaranteed.'},
 'school-19':{'forms':'Gaan uses zijn in this perfect: zij is naar school gegaan. Niet negates that attendance. School has no article in the expression naar school gaan. Learn the auxiliary with this verb; not every movement activity uses zijn.'},
 'leisure-22':{'forms':'Gaan has past ging/gingen. Wij takes gingen, and vroeger elke zomer describes a repeated past habit. Vroeger can also mean earlier in a different context, so the word does not itself force one past tense in every sentence.'},
 'home-29':{'forms':'Schoonmaken is separable: we maken het huis schoon. Its participle is schoongemaakt, with ge between schoon and maakt. Hadden + participle places the completed cleaning before another past reference point; al means already.'},
 'conversation-73':{'forms':'Controleren has participle gecontroleerd, with ge-. Had + gecontroleerd is past perfect: the checking was already complete before a past reference point. De tijd is what was checked; al means already.'},
 'shopping-23':{'forms':'Lijken op means resemble: dat lijkt op goud. Lijken has past leek/leken and participle geleken. Here op belongs to the resemblance expression; compare linking lijken with an adjective in hij lijkt moe, without op.','compare':'English looks like gold means resembles gold, not proof of its material. Dutch uses lijken op here; Dat lijkt goud te zijn instead means That seems to be gold.'},
 'home-30':{'order':'De hond uitlaten is one entire subject constituent naming the activity. Finite is follows that whole phrase in second position, with mijn taak as its complement. De hond alone is not the subject of is.','forms':'Uitlaten stays joined as an infinitive within the subject phrase. Compare finite ik laat de hond uit. Mijn identifies the speaker whose task it is; it has the same form before de- and het-words.'},
 'work-27':{'forms':'Ze means they here and takes beginnen. Volgende maand gives future reference despite the present tense. Met de bouw van het nieuwe gebouw names the activity being started; it is not the grammatical subject. Gebouw is a het-word with definite het, so use nieuwe.'},
 'health-09':{'forms':'Voel is finite. The first je is the subject and the second je is reflexive, referring to that person feeling better. The ordinary present -t of je voelt is omitted before following subject je. Voelen is reflexive for personal well-being here, not in every possible meaning.'},
 'school-20':{'order':'Ze is the subject and plural zullen is finite in second position. De regels is the object before infinitive begrijpen. The complete time phrase na de training follows begrijpen here; the infinitive need not be the last word.','forms':'Zullen expresses the prediction; begrijpen remains an infinitive without te. Regels is plural of de regel. Na de training means after the training, and can also stand before begrijpen.'},
 'administration-05':{'forms':'Wachttijd is a de-word meaning waiting time. Wat asks for that duration; finite is precedes subject de wachttijd. An embedded question uses different order: Ik vraag wat de wachttijd is.'},
 'food-17':{'forms':'Dorst hebben means be thirsty: ik heb dorst. Heb is present, not a perfect auxiliary here. Dorst is a noun without an article in this expression. Compare honger hebben, be hungry, and dorstig zijn, with the adjective dorstig.'},
 'food-18':{'forms':'Hadden is plural past of hebben and agrees with ze meaning they. Geen water negates an unspecified amount of water. Geen is usual with an indefinite or bare noun phrase here, not a replacement for niet before every noun.'},
 'home-32':{'forms':'Ophangen is separable: ze hangen de versieringen op. Plural hangen agrees with ze. Vanmiddag means this afternoon and gives future reference in this plan. Compare perfect ze hebben de versieringen opgehangen.'},
 'transport-35':{'forms':'Gaat agrees with ze meaning she; vliegen stays an infinitive without te after gaan. Volgende zomer identifies next summer and naar Londen gives the destination. Gaan + infinitive can express an intention or a prediction; here it describes a planned flight.'},
 'work-28':{'forms':'Schrijven: ik schrijf, wij schrijven; past schreef/schreven; perfect heb geschreven. We takes plural schrijven. Elke week describes the frequency and rapporten is plural of het rapport.','compare':'The model puts elke week before indefinite rapporten. Other orders can be possible with a different information structure; do not infer that every time phrase must precede every Dutch object.'},
 'transport-37':{'forms':'Gaan uses zijn in the perfect: ze zijn gegaan. Waar ... heen asks about the destination. Plural zijn agrees with ze meaning they. Do not infer that every movement verb always uses zijn; ik heb gefietst describes cycling as an activity.'},
 'home-33':{'order':'Je gaat je kamer opruimen is the main clause: first je is its subject, gaat is finite, je kamer is its object, and opruimen remains an infinitive. Voordat introduces je weggaat, where finite weggaat closes that subordinate clause.','forms':'The je before kamer is possessive your; the other two je forms are subjects meaning you. Opruimen stays joined after gaat. In the subordinate clause weggaan stays joined as finite weggaat; compare the main clause je gaat weg.'},
 'conversation-77':{'forms':'Lijken has past leek/leken. Singular ze leek means she seemed. Verward is a predicate adjective describing her and takes no agreement -e. Compare de verwarde vrouw, where the adjective modifies a noun.','compare':'English She seemed confused uses a linking verb plus an adjective. Dutch leek has the same function here; it is a form of lijken, not zijn or worden.'},
 'home-34':{'forms':'Kweken: ik kweek, wij kweken; past kweekte/kweekten; perfect heb gekweekt. Onze goes with plural groenten. Eigen follows the possessive and means own; it stays eigen here rather than acquiring an extra -e.'},
 'shopping-25':{'forms':'Verkopen is inseparable: ik verkoop, past verkocht/verkochten, perfect heb verkocht. Its unstressed ver- prevents extra ge-. Mijn oude auto takes oude with -e; auto is a de-word. Vorige maand gives the completed sale a past time; Dutch perfect can be used with it.'},
 'transport-38':{'forms':'Komen has past kwam/kwamen and participle gekomen. Plural ze takes kwamen. In te laat, te modifies laat; it is not an infinitive marker. Te laat komen means arrive late.'},
 'shopping-26':{'forms':'De boodschappen doen means do the shopping. Had is the finite past auxiliary and gedaan the irregular participle of doen. Al means already. The past perfect places completion before another past reference point.'},
 'home-35':{'forms':'Zal is finite with ik; repareren stays an infinitive without te. Het is the item repaired, and voor je names the person benefiting. This je is an object after a preposition, not possessive your or the subject.'},
 'shopping-27':{'forms':'Staan has present hij staat; past stond/stonden; perfect heeft gestaan. Achter de toonbank gives the position and in de bakkerij identifies the bakery. Achter precedes the noun phrase in this location expression; this does not ban all Dutch postpositions.'},
 'work-30':{'forms':'Beginnen met werken names the activity started after met. Beginnen te werken is another standard pattern; met is not universally required. Om acht uur gives the clock time; the fixed morning expression means in the morning.'},
 'conversation-79':{'forms':'Graag gedaan is a conventional reply to thanks, literally gladly done. Gedaan is the participle of doen. The short formula has no finite verb; a full explanation is ik heb het graag gedaan. Related expressions can perform different conversational roles.'},
 'home-36':{'forms':'Het bed opmaken means make the bed: ik maak mijn bed op. Opmaken is separable; perfect ik heb mijn bed opgemaakt. Elke ochtend gives the recurring time and follows finite maak in this model, not immediately the subject.'},
 'work-37':{'forms':'Ontmoeten has present wij ontmoeten, with one t between the vowels, but past wij ontmoetten with double t: stem ontmoet + ten. Singular past is ontmoette and participle ontmoet, without extra ge- after ont-. Elkaar means each other, not reflexive ourselves.','meaning':'Reporting that the speaker and another person met yesterday morning; the sentence alone does not say it was their first meeting.'},
 'work-44':{'meaning':'Reporting that the speaker and their group bought an item for a woman. In this gift situation she is the beneficiary; it does not necessarily mean she commissioned the purchase.'},
 'work-50':{'compare':'English naturally uses I will explain for this promise. Dutch uses present leg with future time later; neither the English will nor the Dutch present makes it a past event.'}
})
b['school-19']['mistake']['why']='Gaan takes zijn in this perfect: zij is gegaan. Auxiliary choice follows the particular verb and use, not a universal rule that all movement uses zijn.'
b['leisure-22']['mistake']['why']='This scenario describes a repeated habit in the past, so use plural past gingen. Vroeger can mean earlier in another context; its presence alone does not force a particular tense everywhere.'
b['shopping-23']['mistake']['why']='For resemblance in this model use lijken op goud. Lijken also has other patterns, such as Dat lijkt goud te zijn; op is not required after every occurrence of lijken.'
b['health-09']['mistake']['why']='For this question about personal well-being, use zich voelen and keep both je forms: subject je and reflexive je. Other meanings of voelen need no reflexive, such as ik voel de stof.'
b['school-20']['mistake']['why']='The direct object de regels belongs before begrijpen in this neutral clause. The full time phrase na de training may follow the infinitive, as it does in the model.'
b['food-18']['mistake'].update(alternative=True,why='Geen water is the ordinary negative for an indefinite amount. Niet water can create a contrast such as not water but wine; it is not the neutral meaning intended here.')
b['transport-35']['mistake'].update(alternative=True,why='The model uses neutral time-and-destination placement before vliegen. A full prepositional phrase can also follow the verb bracket, and spoken afterthoughts can add information. Do not interpret this contrast as a universal ban on words after the infinitive.')
b['transport-37']['mistake']['why']='The perfect of gaan uses zijn: waar zijn ze heen gegaan? Learn this verb-specific auxiliary choice rather than assuming every movement activity uses zijn.'
b['work-30']['mistake']['why']='The displayed bare werken does not fit this standard sentence. Use met werken, or the alternative construction te werken: Ik begin om acht uur te werken. Met is not required in every construction with beginnen.'
b['conversation-79']['mistake']['why']='Graag gedaan is the conventional short reply. To add an object, form a full clause such as Ik heb het graag gedaan; simply appending het to the short formula does not work.'

